## dude CLI — all available commands

> Run `dude help` for a live, merged overview.
> Run `dude help <command>` or `dude help <group> <sub>` for flags.

> **How `dude` is provided.** The `dude` command comes from the global launcher
> (`@cubocicloide/dude-launcher`, installed once via `npm i -g`). It resolves the
> CLI + stack versions **this project pins** in `package.json` (lockfile-enforced)
> and runs them — so every contributor and CI use identical tooling. `pnpm install`
> provisions them; the launcher also does it automatically on first run. You can
> equivalently run `pnpm dude <cmd>` from the project root.

### Infrastructure

| Command                   | What it does                                            |
|---------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| `dude up`                 | `docker compose up -d` (start all services)             |
| `dude up --build`         | Rebuild images before starting                          |
| `dude down`               | `docker compose down` (stop + remove containers)        |
| `dude logs [service]`     | Follow logs; omit service to follow all                 |
| `dude shell <service>`    | Interactive shell inside a running container            |

### Code quality

| Command                   | What it does                                                          |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `dude lint`               | Run all stack structural lint checks (naming, layout conventions)     |
| `dude format`             | `ruff format` (backend) + `prettier` (frontend)                       |
| `dude review`             | Run lint + ESLint + API contract review in one pass                   |

### API contract (OpenAPI)

| Command                   | What it does                                                          |
|---------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `dude api sync`           | Fetch OpenAPI spec from the running backend → generate typed client   |
| `dude api review`         | Validate `frontend/src/openapi/` against the saved spec               |

**Workflow**: always run `dude api sync` after changing backend routes, then commit
the updated `frontend/src/openapi/` alongside the route change.

### Testing

| Command                           | What it does                                       |
|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|
| `dude test`                       | Run all available suites (backend + e2e)           |
| `dude test --backend`             | pytest only                                        |
| `dude test --e2e`                 | Playwright + Cucumber only                         |
| `dude test --e2e --headed`        | E2e in a visible browser window                    |
| `dude test --e2e --report`        | E2e with HTML + JSON output to `e2e/reports/`      |

### Security scanning

All scanners run inside Docker — no host-level tool installation required.
New findings cause a non-zero exit; known/accepted findings (baseline) do not.

| Command                                          | What it does                                                  |
|--------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------|
| `dude security scan`                             | Run all scanners; exit 1 if new findings ≥ HIGH               |
| `dude security scan --only bandit,semgrep`       | Run a subset of scanners                                       |
| `dude security scan --min-severity LOW`          | Lower the reporting threshold (default: MEDIUM)               |
| `dude security scan --fail-on CRITICAL`          | Raise the failure threshold (default: HIGH)                   |
| `dude security scan --update-baseline`           | Scan + absorb all findings into the baseline                  |
| `dude security accept`                           | Equivalent to `scan --update-baseline`; use after triage      |
| `dude security verify --rule-id B105,CVE-…`     | Confirm a specific finding has been fixed                     |
| `dude security verify --rule-id B105 --remove-resolved` | Fix confirmed → prune it from the baseline          |

**Scanners**:
- `bandit` — Python AST (backend only)
- `semgrep` — multi-language rules (backend + frontend)
- `trivy-fs` — filesystem: vulns, IaC misconfigs, secrets
- `trivy-image` — container image CVEs (requires built image)

**Baseline workflow**:
1. `dude security scan` — review new findings
2. Triage: fix what needs fixing, accept acceptable risks
3. `dude security accept` — absorbs remaining findings into `security/baseline.json`
4. `git add security/baseline.json && git commit` — commit the baseline update
5. Subsequent scans only flag *new* issues not in the baseline

